module strategy.buyers_pipeline

import std.types { Int, List, NonEmptyStr, Bool }

// THE BUYERS PIPELINE (operator direction 2026-08-29): the operator inputs the parts and
// configurations we need supply for; an LLM scout workflow continuously scans listings
// (eBay and peers), files typed observations, and PROPOSES purchases - and the authority
// split is the load-bearing design: A SCOUT MAY OBSERVE AND PROPOSE, NEVER APPROVE. The
// approval act is the operator's, out of band of the scout (the same reason scaffold
// approval is external to the diff: an agent that can write a proposal can equally write
// a row claiming it was approved). Everything the fragility discipline learned binds
// here: an unverified listing quantity counts as ZERO depth, price is delivered-and-
// qualified (freight, expected yield), and a proposal that exceeds its demand row's
// ceiling is a typed refusal, not a judgment call.

type ConditionClass
  = ListedWorking
  | ListedForParts
  | ListedBiosTestedOnly

// TWO PRICES, TWO FACTS (operator correction 2026-08-30): we do not SET ceilings.
// reference_unit_micro is a dated market quote/estimate - expected to drift UP as our own
// buying consumes depth, and refreshed by executed purchases. viability_ceiling_micro is
// the only refusal wall, and it is DERIVED from a named economics instrument (the price
// at which the build stops surviving), never taste. A listing above reference but under
// viability is an ordinary admissible buy - that spread is exactly what the pipeline is
// solving for: the real cost of converging the fleet against a moving market.
type DemandRow {
  id: NonEmptyStr
  spec: NonEmptyStr
  reference_unit_micro: Int
  viability_ceiling_micro: Int
  viability_source: NonEmptyStr
  wanted_units: Int
  qualification_note: NonEmptyStr
  refusal_conditions: List<NonEmptyStr>
}

// Standing demand, seeded 2026-08-29. Ceilings come from the modeled economics, not
// hope: the Collins ceiling is the analyst's hedge-priced ~$1,000; the EPYC ceiling is
// dollar-per-core parity with the Collins row (~$11/core x 128 cores, delivered).
